=========================================================================

This repository's own code and prose are MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Baladithya Balamurugan
(see LICENSE). This file records every donor whose material a file in this repository
derives from, and — separately — who actually originated the ideas that donor carried.

**This file is a new precedent in this repository.** Before this pass agentic-sdlc shipped
no root LICENSE and no NOTICE, and `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` declared `"license":
"UNLICENSED"` — a direct conflict with the MIT provenance headers already carried inside
`skills/agentic-sdlc/references/skill-authoring.md`, `deep-work-loop.md`, and
`worktree-failclosed-tests.md`. The precedent this file follows is the sibling repository
`pi-lab`'s own root NOTICE (same author, MIT, `pi-lab/NOTICE`): a per-donor record with a
re-resolved commit, the licence grant reproduced where required, an explicit answer to "who
originated the idea" separate from "whose licence covers the bytes", and a what-is/what-is-not
derived pair per donor. `pi-lab/NOTICE` itself states plainly that it is establishing a shape
for the *next* donor to follow rather than an established convention this repository already
had — this file is that next donor's record, for a disjoint, smaller set of derivations.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The provenance rule this file applies
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Where a donor names a third party as the origin of an idea, **the provenance is the third
party, and the donor's licence grant does not launder it.** A permissive licence settles
whether text may be reused. It says nothing about who thought of the thing. Both questions
are answered separately below, for every donor, and a derived file's own header repeats the
answer where a reader of that file will see it. This rule is copied from `pi-lab/NOTICE`
(same author, MIT), which states it was itself written once for a 2026-07-30 harvest fan-in
recorded outside that repository, and treats it as durable house doctrine rather than
reinventing an equivalent.

